#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:4
msgid "Private AI Meeting Notes: The Complete Guide (2026)"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:5
msgid ""
"What 'private' actually means for AI meeting tools — bots, clouds, "
"retention — with a tool-by-tool audit and honest recommendations from a "
"founder in the space."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:6 site/content/links.py:1
msgid "Private AI meeting notes: the complete guide"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:7
msgid ""
"Disclosure: I make MeetMouse, the most private tool on this page — so "
"yes, this guide flatters my product. The facts are checkable, and where a"
" cloud tool is the right call I'll say so."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:9
msgid ""
"\"Private\" gets thrown around loosely. There are three separate "
"questions: does a <em>bot join</em> your call, does your audio <em>leave "
"your machine</em>, and <em>who can access</em> what's stored. Most tools "
"fail two of three. <strong>Granola</strong> fixes the bot. Only "
"<strong>MeetMouse</strong> fixes all three — it's the one tool here where"
" nothing ever leaves your Mac."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:11
msgid ""
"If you handle anything sensitive — candidate interviews, medical or legal"
" conversations, M&amp;A, customer data under NDA, or just 1:1s where "
"people say real things — \"we use an AI notetaker\" is doing a lot of "
"unexamined work. Here's how to actually evaluate privacy in this "
"category, and how each tool scores."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:14
msgid "What is the most private AI meeting notes tool?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:14
msgid ""
"MeetMouse is the only mainstream option where meeting audio never leaves "
"your machine — transcription and AI run entirely on your Mac, with no bot"
" and no account. Granola is the most private cloud notetaker (no bot), "
"but it still processes content with cloud AI."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:15
msgid "Do AI notetakers send your meetings to the cloud?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:15
msgid ""
"Almost all of them, yes — including bot-free ones like Granola, which "
"generate notes with cloud AI. The exception is MeetMouse, which processes"
" everything on-device and works with WiFi off."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:16
msgid "Are AI meeting bots legal in two-party consent states?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:16
msgid ""
"Recording laws vary and this isn't legal advice — but a visible bot plus "
"verbal disclosure is the common practice for recorded calls. On-device "
"tools that transcribe without storing audio recordings sit in a different"
" posture; consult counsel for regulated conversations."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:17
msgid "How do I check if a meeting tool is really private?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:17
msgid ""
"Turn off WiFi mid-meeting. If the tool keeps working, your audio is being"
" processed locally. If it breaks, your meeting is going to someone's "
"servers — go read their retention policy."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:25
msgid "The three questions that matter"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:26
msgid ""
"<strong>1. Does a bot join the call?</strong> A visible recorder changes "
"what people say — and in many contexts (two-party consent states, "
"candidate calls) it's a legal and courtesy question too. Bot-free tools "
"capture system audio on your machine instead."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:27
msgid ""
"<strong>2. Does audio or transcript leave your machine?</strong> \"No "
"bot\" does not mean \"no cloud.\" Most bot-free tools still send meeting "
"content to cloud AI for processing. Once it leaves, you're trusting "
"someone else's retention policy, breach surface, and subpoena response."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:28
msgid ""
"<strong>3. Who can see what's stored?</strong> Team workspaces mean your "
"calls are readable by admins and managers. Sales tools pipe them into "
"CRMs. Ask: who, besides me, can pull up this conversation?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:34
msgid "Tool-by-tool: the privacy audit"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:37 site/build.py:807
msgid "Bot joins?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:37
msgid "Content leaves your machine?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:37
msgid "Stored where?"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:38
#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:39
msgid "Yes — audio + transcript"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:38
msgid "Otter's cloud workspace"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:39
msgid "Fireflies' cloud + your CRM"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:40
msgid "Yes — recording + transcript"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:41
msgid "Yes — recording"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:41
#: site/content/compare.py:1
msgid "tl;dv's cloud"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:42
msgid "Yes — audio processed in cloud"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:43
msgid "Yes — content sent to cloud AI for notes"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:44
msgid "No — transcription + AI on your Mac"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:44
msgid "Your Mac only"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:47
msgid ""
"Exact retention and access policies change — always check the vendor's "
"current terms for anything regulated. The structural facts above (bot vs "
"no bot, cloud vs on-device) are what they can't change without rebuilding"
" the product."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:53
msgid "If you need cloud features anyway"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:54
msgid ""
"Sometimes the archive or the CRM sync <em>is</em> the job, and the right "
"answer is a cloud tool used deliberately: turn on the shortest retention "
"window offered, restrict workspace access, tell participants they're "
"recorded, and keep genuinely sensitive calls off the tool entirely. "
"Fathom free is the best value in that world; <a href=\"/compare"
"/meetmouse-vs-otter\">Otter</a> if you need search; <a href=\"/compare"
"/meetmouse-vs-fireflies\">Fireflies</a> if the CRM machine earns its "
"keep."
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:60
msgid "If private actually means private"
msgstr ""

#: site/articles/blog__private-ai-meeting-notes.html:61
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The test is simple: <strong>turn off WiFi. Does the tool still "
"work?</strong> Every notetaker above fails it. <a "
"href=\"/\">MeetMouse</a> passes because there's nothing to send: an on-"
"device speech model transcribes, a local LLM writes the summary and next "
